:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "22 – 23 August 2004"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/786"} "Olympiako Gymnastirio Ano Liosion, Ano Liossia"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/134346"} "12 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Round-robin pools, winner of each pool advanced to semi-finals. Runners-up in each pool advanced to classification round for 5/6 places."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This was the only 2004 women’s wrestling event in which the favorite did not win. That role settled on Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/107576"} "Kyoko Hamaguchi"] ", who had won the 2002-03 World Championships. Her main contenders were expected to be China’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/107574"} "Wang Xu"] ", runner-up at the 2002 Worlds, American " [:a {:href "/athletes/107580"} "Toccara Montgomery"] ", runner-up at the 2003 Worlds, and Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/107578"} "Christine Nordhagen"] ", World Champion in 2000 and three times a World Champion as a middleweight. Only Wang and Hamaguchi got through the pools to the knockout phase, where they were drawn against each other in the semi-final, with Wang winning, 6-4, in a controversial decision where the Japanese coaches thought two points awarded to Wang should have gone to Hamaguchi. Wang’s finals opponent was Russian " [:a {:href "/athletes/107575"} "Gyuzel Manyurova"] ", who had recently won the 2004 European title, but she was no match for Wang, who won the gold medal, 7-2. Hamaguchi did come back to win the bronze medal, which she would repeat at Beijing in 2008. At only 18-years-old, Wang’s victory made her the youngest ever Olympic wrestling champion."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right", :data-original-title "Classification Points", 